I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the framework’s analysis/scoring guidance. Then I’ll build a falsifiable candidate ledger, screen phases against the full peak set, and only refine the surviving inventory in GSAS-II.

The context narrows the chemistry to Zn–W–O after a 700 °C firing, but it does not justify assuming pure ZnWO₄: residual ZnO/WO₃, polymorphs, and off-stoichiometric intermediates remain live hypotheses. I’m now inventorying the supplied structure library and extracting the observed peak families before nominating candidates.

The first pattern check strongly supports a wolframite-type phase: its diagnostic doublets and families occur near 18.99°, 23.7–24.6°, 30.52/30.76°, 36.44°, 38.37°, and 53.64°. The exact Zn–W–O entries are deliberately absent from the supplied library, so I’m testing all 15 available AWO₄ prototypes after Zn substitution, alongside every usable ZnO and WO₃ polymorph; I will not treat an unimportable or physically broken source as a rejection.

The FeWO₄ parent is decisively the best topology by profile, but its published coordinates contain implausibly short 1.50 Å W–O bonds, so I’m keeping it only as a diagnostic parent. I found broader one- and two-substitution options—including ZnMoO₄, CoMoO₄, MnMoO₄, MgMoO₄, and columbite/wolframite oxides—and am framework-matching those now to obtain a physically sound starting geometry rather than refining through a bad source.

The physically sound CoMoO₄-derived P2/c model now explains the major pattern and refines stably; the scale/background-only Rwp was 40.7%, dropping to 29.9% after only the four symmetry-independent cell terms and common angular zero. This is still an inventory trial—not the final fit. I’m next separating instrumental/specimen broadening from coherent residual peaks, then testing ZnO and each viable WO₃ polymorph on the same saved project.

The first precursor trials are not yet conclusive: several raw COD CIFs carried problematic atom/ADP encodings that made GSAS-II abort, so those branches are “unknown,” not rejected. Wurtzite ZnO only helped after an implausibly large/unstable cell shift; zincblende gave a small stable gain but is chemically uneconomical. A monoclinic WO₃ source gave a stronger positive scale, yet that particular record has a 0.46 Å O–O contact and failed its cell trial. I’m repeating every affected branch with oxidation labels and ADPs normalized while preserving topology and occupancies.

The repaired trials resolve the main inventory: monoclinic WO₃ (COD 2311041, a valid alternate setting) gives a stable ~20 wt% contribution and lowers Rwp from 29.88% to 21.64% with only a ~0.3% volume change. Triclinic and orthorhombic WO₃ also absorb some intensity, but their cell refinements are nonconvergent or much less effective; they remain unknown/rejected only where the test was stable. I’m now checking structural equivalence among those WO₃ settings and retesting ZnO against the accepted two-phase model, where false minor-phase gains should collapse.

The three-phase inventory survives common refinement: wolframite ZnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and wurtzite ZnO. Rocksalt ZnO collapses; zincblende falls to ~0.3 wt%; wurtzite remains ~3 wt% with only ~0.35% cell adjustment, so it is retained provisionally. Common profile plus phase-isotropic strain terms are physical and stable (Rwp 20.12%); size refinement added no supported improvement and was rolled back. I’m now refining only symmetry-allowed coordinates, beginning with the eight independent wolframite coordinates.

The dominant remaining effect is now clear: a single March–Dollase term for ZnWO₄ along [100] refines to 0.750 and lowers Rwp from 19.53% to 8.45%, while all four alternative axes are much worse. This term is within the physically allowed range and stable. Surface roughness was boundary-hitting/correlated and was rejected; common phase Uiso refinements stay near 0.01 Å². I’m testing texture on each minor phase and then one supported anisotropic broadening term at a time.

The phase inventory is now narrowed to wolframite ZnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and wurtzite ZnO. I’m doing the final closure checks now: equivalent WO₃ models and plausible Zn-rich oxide prototypes, followed by a clean GSAS-II replay and artifact export.

The strongest unexplained residual is an overlapped 41° family, not a set of new isolated peaks. I’m testing whether it is better explained by an alternative WO₃ setting/coordinate model before allowing any extra phase into the inventory.

The broader prototype screen found a few Zn₃WO₆ analogues that can place intensity near the 41° residual, plus weak Zn₂WO₅ and fractional tungsten-bronze matches. I’m now requiring them to survive an actual common-project GSAS-II scale/cell trial; profile coincidence alone is not enough.

The late-stage tests favor a smoother 12-term background, but not extra structural freedom: WO₃ oxygen refinements either worsened the fit or required implausibly large shifts, and instrument U was strongly correlated with microstrain/profile X. I’m retaining the simpler structural model and stabilizing only the supported background change.

The controlled shortlist closes the principal topology question: the selected P2/c wolframite analogue reaches the correct cells and phase balance, while triclinic, scheelite, and C2/c alternatives remain near 50% Rwp; the Fe-derived P2/c model is geometrically poor and its cell trial does not converge. Next I’m quantifying each retained phase by omission before export.

All three exported CIFs pass the physical screen: full occupancies, sensible densities, and shortest contacts of 1.76 Å (ZnWO₄), 1.72 Å (WO₃), and 1.98 Å (ZnO). I’m now replaying `final.gpx` from saved atomic/profile parameters, ignoring its cached calculated curve, to generate the submitted profile and verify consistency.

The required deliverables already pass replay and physical checks. Before handing off, I’m doing one last sensitivity audit on late-stage peak broadening and the dominant wolframite coordinate basin, because the remaining 41° mismatch is larger than counting noise and could otherwise bias the minor-phase fraction.

Completed and independently replay-verified.

- ZnWO₄ (wolframite): 74.60 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 18.91 wt%
- Wurtzite ZnO: 6.49 wt%
- Rwp: 7.9024%; Rp: 4.3751%; GOF: 3.496

No reliable fourth crystalline phase was found. The main remaining uncertainty is an overlapping residual near 41.35° 2θ; realistic fraction uncertainty is approximately 1–2 percentage points.

